---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyBudget from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyBudget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="budget-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyBudget client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Where 50/30/20 comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Elizabeth Warren and Amelia Warren Tyagi made the split popular in <em>All Your Worth</em>
      (2005). Its strength is that it is coarse. Three buckets are easy to remember and easy to check
      against a bank statement in five minutes. People still use it twenty years later, while
      forty-line spreadsheet budgets are abandoned within a month.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>50% needs: what you would still owe in a bad month.</li>
      <li>30% wants: all you could cut without a crisis.</li>
      <li>20% savings and extra debt payments: money for future security.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Take-home pay, not salary</h2>
    <p>
      The percentages apply to the money that reaches your account. Gross salary makes each target too
      high by about your marginal tax rate, and the budget then cannot be followed. If your pension or
      health premium is deducted before you are paid, it is outside this budget: you already save that
      money, so do not count it twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are paid weekly or fortnightly, enter the amount and the frequency. The budget calculator
      annualises at 52 or 26 periods and divides by twelve. This spreads the two "extra" pay packets of
      a fortnightly schedule across the year. If you budget a fortnightly wage as "×2 per month", you
      lose a month of income each year.
    </p>

    <h2>The needs/wants line, honestly drawn</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Need</th>
            <th>Want</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Rent or mortgage</td><td>A bigger place than you need</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Utilities, basic phone</td><td>Streaming, premium data plan</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Groceries</td><td>Restaurants, takeaway, coffee out</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Transport to work</td><td>A car payment above what a cheaper car would cost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insurance, childcare</td><td>Gym, hobbies, holidays</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Minimum debt payments</td><td>None</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Two rules settle most questions. First, the minimum payment on a debt is a need; any amount above
      it is savings, because it reduces future interest. Second, if a category can be reduced but not
      removed (food, transport), put the basic version in needs and the difference in wants.
    </p>

    <h2>When 50% of your pay does not cover the rent</h2>
    <p>
      In an expensive city, this is normal. The rule is still useful with a different split. Use the
      60/20/20 preset and read the overrun as information: it shows how much housing takes from
      everything else. Use that number when you think about moving, sharing or negotiating pay.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not reduce savings to keep wants. Savings at zero is the failure that this framework makes
      visible.
    </p>

    <h2>What the variance table is for</h2>
    <p>
      The variance table compares the targets with the line items you enter, so you can see if you
      overspend. A positive variance means the category has room left; a negative one means the plan
      is already over the target before the month starts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bring the "left unallocated" figure to zero. Income without a category is spent without a
      decision, which is the usual reason for "I don't know where my money goes".
    </p>

    <h2>A workable first pass</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your take-home pay and how often you are paid.</li>
      <li>Get the last full month of bank and card statements.</li>
      <li>Add one line per recurring payment, and tag each as need, want or savings.</li>
      <li>Add one "everyday spending" want line for variable spending.</li>
      <li>Read the variance. If the mismatch is structural, adjust the split, not the facts.</li>
      <li>Export the plan to CSV or PDF and check it again next month.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Related targets worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Three months of needs: the standard starter emergency fund, shown above from your needs target.
      </li>
      <li>
        Savings rate: savings ÷ take-home pay. The rule is twenty per cent; how far above that you go
        decides how fast you reach financial independence.
      </li>
      <li>
        Housing ratio: lenders test 28% of gross income, which is about 35–40% of take-home pay.
        Cross-check with the home affordability calculator.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your numbers stay on your device</h2>
    <p>
      This page has no account, server or analytics. Line items are saved only if you turn on saving,
      only in this browser, and the delete button next to the switch removes them immediately. The
      CSV, Excel and PDF exports are made locally, and the page works with the network turned off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
